  "query_preview": "..\n\nThrough Tuesday...\n\nBreezy westerly winds along the western foothills and near the \nDakotas border will decrease into the evening hours. There is a \nlow chance (20% or less) of patchy fog along the Musselshell and \nlower Yellowstone river valleys late tonight into Thursday morning\nwith dewpoint depressions approaching zero and light winds. \nTroughing developing along the West Coast and high pressure over \nthe central US will keep mainly dry conditions and seasonable \ntemperatures in place into Friday. Energy advecting in from the \nsouthwest brings a return of a low to moderate chance (20-40%) of \nsnow showers to the western mountains late Friday into Saturday. \nHigh temperatures are forecast from the 40s to 50s most days, \nwarmest on Saturday. \n\nHeading into early next week (Sunday and Monday), upper troughing\nand an associated area of low pressure look to move inland into \nthe Rockies, bringing the potential for a return of unsettled \nconditions to the region. There remains uncertainty in the track \nof the low, and with that, the potential for cooler temperatures, \nprecipitation amounts and wind. Some ensembles show a more \npronounced area of low pressure, tracking it through Wyoming, and \nwith that the potential for more precipitation (rain and mountain \nsnow). While other ensembles show a weaker low, bringing less \nprecipitation, but more wind to the region. Will need to monitor \nthe system over the coming days, but cooler conditions, moderate \nchances for precipitation (40-60%) and wind are all possible. \nDrier conditions look to move back into the region for the middle \nof next week. STP",
